From: clasy...@gmail.com <Unknown>
Date: Sunday, September 16, 2018 at 8:44:33 AM UTC-5
Subject: Internal reorganization of the entire PEPS package directory 
structure and some features added.
To: pepswin <Unknown>


While at the RICM Learning Lab, I was showing P?S/8 running on a real 8/I 
on DECtape on TU55 and TC01, RX01 on a real PDP-12 and on PEPS on the same 
DECtape system.  PEPS is finally nearly at the "I'm done for now" stage and 
not expecting to add any more nifty features for now.

The directories essentially hatched and were getting to be pesky in many 
ways, now are organized in a much more rational way, and consistent with 
some new features implemented in SIMH itself.  Some of this stuff I used 
had to be discovered because the documentation of SIMH is rather dreadful 
in spots.  [This ranges from undocumented features that do work to 
documented features that don't work].  I exploited some to allow some new 
features, such as your choice of editor for all source-level host-provided 
file editing using your choice of Notepad, EditPad Lite 7 or Notepad++ 
available either by slightly modifying each relevant command file or more 
elegantly relying on an optional environment variable.  If the enrinment 
variable exists and is properly formatted, the designated editor will be 
used.  You can always change using three new proveded setup options in an 
auxiliary window called Setup.

It's a lot of little things, and they all work except for one that has to 
be tested.  It sets up the optional auto-arrange disable and user-settible 
enable it as well as separately align to grid.  This is standard in Windows 
XP and Vista, and was removed in Windows 7 [but only the "outer" layer; a 
registry patch wakes it up.  It works fine in Windows 7, and where I lifted 
it from suggests it should also work in Windows 8/8.1 as well.  It is not 
clear what happens in Windows 10, so I have to test this out in those two 
systems as well.  [I do have done the code to know it is running on XP or 
Vista and just exit working now.]

The default serving suggestion of the Commands windows uses medium-sized 
icons and a suggested layout that needs this.  There are so many icons you 
need order and some gaps for it to make sense, etc. [Of course you are free 
to change it into details mode, but you lose the ability for it to be 
sorted the way you wish, however, that said there is a trick:

You can rename each of them as in the following:

Edit Current Program can be changed to

01 Edit Current Program

and

Send Current Program

can become

02 Send Current Program

etc.

Then sort by name forces the issue [if you use either List view or Details 
view.  It is not suitable to provide this change if medium icon view is 
used as it throws off the readability of the icons for no purpose, but a 
lot in Windows is personal preference.  My STRONG RECOMMENDATION for the 
key command center of the package stay in the provided form; I've tried all 
of these variations, and this mode is clearly the easiest to use this 
package; ymmv, but Windows allows every window to be different, etc.

Thus, soon to get back to the documentation, which is now a bit more 
obsolete because of all of the new features.  Part of the change is taking 
out of the equation some icons only seldom needed.  They are now instead 
revealed by clicking on one of the main commands icons to reveal another 
smaller window for the rest.  The goal is to avoid clutter, etc.

Look for an announcement of at least the working copy once I test out the 
auto-arrange code on 8 and 10.

cjl

